spring is traditionally one of the  driest seasons when it comes to new  games but this isn't necessarily a bad  thing keeping in line with the  expectations there weren't a lot of new  games in April but there were some gems  that rose to the top going toe-to-toe  with a big-budget game and totally  holding their own box boy on the 3ds  doesn't look like much from the outside  but it's simple mechanics and puzzles  expand gradually over time testing your  intellect just enough to consistently  deliver a strong sense of satisfaction  affordable space adventures also caught  our attention by making great use of the  Wii U's gamepad to facilitate teamwork  as you explore the beautiful and  mysterious far-off reaches of deep space  crypt of the necrodancer took a dungeon  crawling to new depths melding roguelike  elements with mechanics from rhythm  games offering an experience unlike  anything that we played before though it  technically came out in March we  reviewed pillars of eternity in April so  the impressive amaj to baldur's gate was  also among our short list of candidates  but these games are proof that any time  of the year can be a good time for games  as usual it was difficult to choose our  game at the Month for April that the  award ultimately goes to the robust  Mortal Kombat X the core of the mortal  combat experience remains largely the  same in MK x you fight against an  opponent in a violent bout replete with  blood moans of pain and over-the-top  finishing moves alongside some fresh  faces classic characters from the past  return true to form when an extensive  story mode the details their struggles  the fighting system has  never felt so fluid either and the added  depth of character variations makes one  character feel like three giving you  more to explore and learn and more for  your opponents to worry about Mortal  Kombat X delivers the series  characteristic violence in such a  captivating and entertaining way leaning  on absurdity wherever possible to great  effect you cringe at the sight and sound  of flesh tearing and bones breaking but  you can't help but laugh when the act is  punctuated with the timely quip MKX is a  great fighting experience with deep  mechanics a big game with no shortage of  challenges and modes to explore and a  tour de force of brutality that's  enhanced by the game's impressive  visuals the rest of our candidates and  no doubt great games but the honour of  game of the month belongs to m KX
 

